6.  SHEEP-D2 [proved]: no branch v >= 6 can be second-to-last
      Reduce each criterion modulo A_v (a necessary condition), using
      2^(v+1) = -1, so <2> = {2^i}_(i<=v) u {A_v - 2^i}_(i<=v), and the
      exponent parity of each element is well defined because
      2^(v+1) = -1 forces ord_(A_v)(2) to be EVEN.
        H_0 target:  3B_v - 1 = 3*2^v + 3v - 1 = 2^v + 3v - 2  (mod A_v)
        H_1 target:  5B_v + 2 = 5*2^v + 5v + 2 = 2^v + 5v      (mod A_v)
      (using 3*2^v = 2^(v+1) + 2^v = 2^v - 1 and 5*2^v = 2^(v+2) + 2^v
       = 2^v - 2.)  Each target must be +-2^i, giving two gap cases:
        H_0 case A:  2^v + 3v - 2 = 2^i    -- impossible for v >= 4,
                     since 2^v < 2^v + 3v - 2 < 2^(v+1)
        H_0 case B:  2^i = 2^v - 3v + 3    -- impossible for v >= 5,
                     since 2^(v-1) < 2^v - 3v + 3 < 2^v
        H_1 case A:  2^v + 5v = 2^i        -- impossible for v >= 5
        H_1 case B:  2^i = 2^v - 5v + 1    -- impossible for v >= 6;
                     v = 5 gives 32 - 25 + 1 = 8 = 2^3, a REAL hit
      so v >= 6 is excluded and v <= 5 is finite, checked directly.
      gap inequalities re-checked for every v < 6000: 0 violations
      v = 5 exception confirmed: 2^5 - 5*5 + 1 = 8 = 2^3
      and the criteria themselves, 400 <= v < 3000: survivors []

